// The terms page and the two legal checkboxes. The page is plain words in the
// hush voice; the checkboxes are the operative part — both are enforced
// server-side and recorded (agreements.go), the labels just tell the truth
// about what checking them means.
package main

// agreeBox is the 18+/terms checkbox the three signup doors share. Required
// client-side AND server-side (agreed + recordAgreement) — the HTML attribute
// is a courtesy, the handler is the rule.
templ agreeBox() {
	<label class="hush-agree">
		<input type="checkbox" name="agree18" required/>
		<span>i am 18 or older and i accept the <a class="hush-link" href="/terms" target="_blank">terms</a>.</span>
	</label>
}

// certBox2257 is the certification that rides every image upload — the wall
// form and the camera page both carry it. id'd so camera.js can read it.
templ certBox2257(id string) {
	<label class="hush-agree">
		<input type="checkbox" id={ id } name="cert2257" required/>
		<span>i certify under 18 U.S.C. § 2257 that every person depicted is 18 or older, and that i am the producer of this image or hold the records proving it. see the <a class="hush-link" href="/terms" target="_blank">terms</a>.</span>
	</label>
}

// termsPage is /terms: the rules of the wall, written the way the wall talks.
// Short sentences, no legalese it doesn't need, and the legally load-bearing
// parts (18+, the license, zero tolerance, section 2257, what we keep) intact
// underneath the voice. contact is the operator address (HUSH_ADMIN_EMAIL),
// shown when set.
templ termsPage(contact string) {
	@shell("hush — terms") {
		<div class="hush-top hush-terms">
			<header class="hush-head">
				<h1 class="hush-title"><a class="hush-home" href="/">hush</a></h1>
				<p class="hush-sub">terms of service</p>
			</header>
			<section class="hush-terms-body">
				<h2>1. what this is</h2>
				<p>hush is a quiet wall. you post into it and it keeps what it likes. being here means you accept these terms. if they don't suit you, the door is where you left it.</p>
				<h2>2. eighteen</h2>
				<p>you must be <strong>18 or older</strong> to be here with an account. to post, to vote, to flag. checking the box means you are. if you aren't, your account ends the moment we notice, and everything you posted goes with it.</p>
				<h2>3. getting in</h2>
				<p>nobody is owed a seat. you apply, you claim an open one, or someone spends an invite on you, and whoever invited you answers for you. we can close any account at any time, for any reason or none. the wall is anonymous to everyone else. it is not anonymous to us.</p>
				<h2>4. what you post</h2>
				<p>it stays yours. posting gives us permission to host it, copy it, resize it, and show it, because that is what a wall does. only post what is yours to post.</p>
				<h2>5. what never goes up</h2>
				<p>nothing illegal. nothing sexual involving anyone under 18, ever. <strong>that one isn't curation, it's evidence: preserved and reported to the national center for missing &amp; exploited children and to law enforcement, every time.</strong> no doxxing, no threats, no spam, nothing posted to hurt someone. the wall is curated; anything can come down for any reason.</p>
				<h2>6. images</h2>
				<p>every image you put up carries a certification, and we keep it: that everyone in the picture was 18 or older when it was made, and that you made it or hold the records under 18 U.S.C. § 2257 and 28 C.F.R. part 75 that prove it. if you can't stand behind that, don't touch the shutter.</p>
				<h2>7. what we keep</h2>
				<p>the wall shows no names and no numbers. behind it we keep what an operator needs: the ip, rough location, and browser attached to every post, vote, flag, signup, and certification. your email, if you offered one. how the wall gets used. we look at it, we use it to keep the place alive, and we hand it over when the law makes us. we don't sell it.</p>
				<h2>8. no promises</h2>
				<p>the wall is what it is, as is, when it's up. if it eats your post, your standing, or your invites, that's the weather. our entire liability to you is one hundred dollars or what you paid us, and you paid us nothing.</p>
				<h2>9. endings</h2>
				<p>we can change these terms, or turn the lights off, whenever. staying after a change means yes. sections 4 through 8 outlive your account.</p>
				if contact != "" {
					<h2>10. contact</h2>
					<p>the operator reads { contact }.</p>
				}
			</section>
			@backToWall()
		</div>
	}
}
